{
  "image_name": "4df520cb362a2e1a27f2a6a22d9279cd.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how ion-channel control, contractility, and protein acetylation are linked to cardiac rhythm. At the cell membrane, sodium channel Nav1.5 and calcium channel Cav1.2/Cav3.2 regulate depolarization, calcium entry, and repolarization. Sodium influx appears to initiate depolarization, while calcium influx supports a calcium-dependent contraction of the myofibrils; subsequent calcium and sodium outflow is associated with repolarization and relaxation.\n\nInside the cell, the sarcomere-like myosin structures are shown switching between relaxed and contracted states, emphasizing that altered calcium handling feeds directly into contraction. Beneath this, the diagram connects disrupted contractile regulation to arrhythmia.\n\nA second major theme is post-translational and transcriptional regulation. Nav1.5 and α-tubulin are shown as being modified by opposing enzyme pairs: CBP versus SIRT1 for Nav1.5, and αTAT1 versus HDAC6 for α-tubulin. Reduced α-tubulin acetylation appears to promote α-tubulin degradation, which is linked to arrhythmia. In the nucleus, HDAC1/HDAC2 and NRSF are depicted repressing genes including Cacna1h and Cacna2d2, which encode calcium-channel components. Those gene products then feed back to the membrane channels. Overall, the image presents arrhythmia as a consequence of combined electrical, mechanical, and epigenetic dysregulation.",
